1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:04,070
Light can be trapped. Glass fibres carry it under oceans,

2
00:00:04,110 --> 00:00:08,430
and photonic chips route it through wires half a micron wide.

3
00:00:08,500 --> 00:00:10,987
This ring, a few microns across, sorts colours.

4
00:00:11,027 --> 00:00:14,117
Send in several, and most pass straight by…

5
00:00:14,187 --> 00:00:15,871
…but one colour drops into the ring,

6
00:00:15,911 --> 00:00:18,628
circles it, and leaves through the other wire.

7
00:00:18,698 --> 00:00:22,573
How do you trap light in a wire? And why do only certain colours fit?

8
00:00:24,693 --> 00:00:26,699
Start with a strip of glass, the core,

9
00:00:26,739 --> 00:00:31,077
surrounded by material of lower refractive index, the cladding.

10
00:00:31,147 --> 00:00:34,531
A ray that meets the wall at a shallow angle can't escape.

11
00:00:34,571 --> 00:00:37,977
It's totally reflected, and zigzags along the core.

12
00:00:38,047 --> 00:00:42,475
But light is a wave. The zigzag is really two plane waves crossing:

13
00:00:42,515 --> 00:00:44,614
one heading up, one heading down.

14
00:00:44,684 --> 00:00:47,199
Together they form a standing wave across the core,

15
00:00:47,239 --> 00:00:48,984
which travels along it.

16
00:00:49,054 --> 00:00:53,761
But not every angle works. Follow the wave up across the core and back:

17
00:00:53,801 --> 00:00:57,430
it must return in step with itself, or the bounces cancel.

18
00:00:57,500 --> 00:01:00,816
So the round-trip phase must be a whole number of turns,

19
00:01:00,856 --> 00:01:05,179
2πm. Slide the angle, and only a few angles click into place.

20
00:01:05,249 --> 00:01:08,425
These are the modes. Each keeps its shape as it travels,

21
00:01:08,465 --> 00:01:11,905
with tails that fade into the cladding: evanescent waves.

22
00:01:12,725 --> 00:01:15,998
There's a cleaner way to find modes, with linear algebra.

23
00:01:16,068 --> 00:01:19,950
A mode is a shape E(x) that travels as e^(iβz).

24
00:01:19,990 --> 00:01:24,943
Put that into the wave equation, and out comes an eigenvalue problem.

25
00:01:25,013 --> 00:01:29,801
Sample x on a grid. The second derivative becomes a tridiagonal matrix,

26
00:01:29,841 --> 00:01:33,233
and the index profile adds to its diagonal.

27
00:01:33,303 --> 00:01:35,587
Its eigenvectors are the mode shapes.

28
00:01:35,627 --> 00:01:39,917
Its eigenvalues give each mode's propagation constant, β.

29
00:01:39,987 --> 00:01:42,370
And each effective index, β/k₀,

30
00:01:42,410 --> 00:01:45,788
lies between the cladding and core indices.

31
00:01:45,858 --> 00:01:50,309
Any input is a sum of modes, each advancing its own phase.

32
00:01:50,349 --> 00:01:53,196
It's the angular spectrum method from episode one,

33
00:01:53,236 --> 00:01:55,294
with modes in place of plane waves.

34
00:01:55,364 --> 00:01:58,494
Launch an off-centre spot into a wide guide.

35
00:01:58,534 --> 00:02:00,311
The modes beat into a kaleidoscope,

36
00:02:00,351 --> 00:02:03,604
then fall back in step, and the spot reappears.

37
00:02:04,424 --> 00:02:07,248
Now place two identical guides side by side,

38
00:02:07,288 --> 00:02:11,124
so close that their tails overlap, and launch light into one.

39
00:02:11,194 --> 00:02:15,319
It drifts across into the other guide, then comes back, again and again.

40
00:02:15,389 --> 00:02:19,470
Why? The pair has modes of its own: one symmetric,

41
00:02:19,510 --> 00:02:24,706
one antisymmetric, with propagation constants β + κ and β − κ.

42
00:02:24,776 --> 00:02:29,394
Light in one guide is the sum of the two. One slowly falls behind,

43
00:02:29,434 --> 00:02:32,333
and after a distance π/2κ it has flipped sign:

44
00:02:32,373 --> 00:02:35,404
now the sum sits in the other guide.

45
00:02:35,474 --> 00:02:39,436
In the basis of the two guides, that's a 2×2 rotation:

46
00:02:39,476 --> 00:02:44,090
amplitude cos κz in one, i sin κz in the other.

47
00:02:44,160 --> 00:02:46,853
Stop halfway and you have a 50:50 splitter,

48
00:02:46,893 --> 00:02:51,522
the building block of interferometers, switches and optical processors.

49
00:02:52,342 --> 00:02:56,342
To store light, bend a guide into a ring beside a straight one.

50
00:02:56,382 --> 00:02:59,502
On every pass, a little light leaks across into the ring.

51
00:02:59,572 --> 00:03:01,306
Each round trip adds another copy,

52
00:03:01,346 --> 00:03:05,592
rotated by the round-trip phase, and a little weaker than the last.

53
00:03:05,662 --> 00:03:10,237
Off resonance, the copies curl into a small spiral, and mostly cancel.

54
00:03:10,307 --> 00:03:12,924
On resonance, when the ring fits a whole number of wavelengths,

55
00:03:12,964 --> 00:03:17,134
they line up into one long arrow. The light piles up.

56
00:03:18,004 --> 00:03:19,845
Sweep the colour, and the stored light

57
00:03:19,885 --> 00:03:21,778
spikes at evenly spaced resonances.

58
00:03:21,818 --> 00:03:24,574
That's what the ring in the opening did.

59
00:03:25,394 --> 00:03:27,826
How sharp is each spike? That's set by loss:

60
00:03:27,866 --> 00:03:31,515
the more round trips the light survives, the narrower the peak.

61
00:03:31,585 --> 00:03:33,797
We measure it with the quality factor:

62
00:03:33,837 --> 00:03:36,404
the wavelength over the peak's width,

63
00:03:36,444 --> 00:03:41,496
Q = λ/Δλ. High Q means light stays stored for a long time.

64
00:03:41,566 --> 00:03:43,710
Now watch the light that goes straight past.

65
00:03:43,750 --> 00:03:46,235
It meets the light leaking back out of the ring,

66
00:03:46,275 --> 00:03:48,803
which on resonance comes out flipped.

67
00:03:48,873 --> 00:03:51,434
So the through port dims. When the ring leaks

68
00:03:51,474 --> 00:03:54,383
out exactly as fast as it loses light inside,

69
00:03:54,423 --> 00:03:57,311
the two cancel completely: critical coupling.

70
00:03:57,981 --> 00:04:01,583
Couple more weakly, or more strongly, and the dip gets shallower.

71
00:04:02,403 --> 00:04:05,610
On a computer, each idea takes just a few lines.

72
00:04:05,680 --> 00:04:10,746
Modes: build the tridiagonal matrix and call an eigenvalue solver.

73
00:04:10,786 --> 00:04:13,396
Make the window wide enough for the tails,

74
00:04:13,436 --> 00:04:14,911
or its edges squeeze the modes.

75
00:04:14,981 --> 00:04:20,448
Propagation: the beam propagation method, BPM. It alternates two steps:

76
00:04:20,488 --> 00:04:23,637
diffraction, an FFT as in episode one,

77
00:04:23,677 --> 00:04:26,119
and refraction, a thin phase screen.

78
00:04:26,189 --> 00:04:29,973
Repeat, slice after slice. That's how the coupler was computed.

79
00:04:30,043 --> 00:04:34,680
Resonators: add up the round trips, a geometric series, in one line.

80
00:04:34,750 --> 00:04:38,683
The catch: BPM only marches forward, at gentle angles.

81
00:04:38,723 --> 00:04:42,789
Fine for couplers, but wrong for tight bends and reflections.

82
00:04:43,609 --> 00:04:47,469
Trapped light settles into modes, the eigenvectors of the cross-section.

83
00:04:47,509 --> 00:04:51,385
A resonator stores light where round trips add in phase.

84
00:04:51,455 --> 00:04:55,844
But these shortcuts assume gentle, forward-going light. Sharp bends,

85
00:04:55,884 --> 00:04:58,113
gratings and nanostructures break that,

86
00:04:58,153 --> 00:05:01,368
and then you need Maxwell's equations themselves.

87
00:05:01,438 --> 00:05:03,928
How does a computer solve them? Next time.

